  "notes": [
    "Definitions used: Revenue = Homebuilding revenues + Mortgage banking fees (both from NVR consolidated statements of income); EBIT constructed as operating income excluding net interest effects from the same statement (so EBIT is a modeled derivation, not a single reported line item).",
    "Working capital (NWC) construction is simplified from year-end balance sheet lines: (Receivables + Homebuilding inventory + Contract land deposits + Mortgage loans held for sale) minus (Homebuilding accounts payable + Accrued expenses/other liabilities + Customer deposits + Mortgage banking accounts payable/other liabilities). This excludes cash/restricted cash and intentionally excludes lease liabilities and long-term debt due to lack of explicit current/non-current split.",
    "Forecast drivers are base-case judgments (no explicit 5-year quantitative guidance found in the filings); they are designed to be sober and chain correctly off FY2025 revenue.",
    "No NOL disclosure was located in the FY2025 Form 10-K text search; NOL fields are set to null to avoid inventing balances."
  ],
  "presentation": {
    "as_of_date": "2026-03-20",
    "catalysts": [
      "Sustained improvement in mortgage affordability and interest-rate expectations supporting new orders and backlog replenishment.",
      "Margin stabilization as 2026 backlog settles with less lot-cost pressure and fewer deposit impairments.",
      "Continued capital returns (share repurchases) if NVR maintains strong operating cash generation through the cycle."
    ],
    "company_overview": [
      "NVR operates in two business segments: homebuilding and mortgage banking. Homebuilding revenues are driven by settlement of pre-sold homes; mortgage banking originates mortgage loans for homebuyers and sells loans into secondary markets.",
      "The company\u2019s homebuilding brands (Ryan Homes, NVHomes, Heartland Homes) operate across multiple U.S. markets (37 metropolitan areas in 16 states plus Washington, D.C.).",
      "NVR uses a land- and asset-light lot acquisition strategy (e.g., lot purchase agreements with forfeitable deposits) and relies on independent subcontractors for construction."
    ],
    "current_context": [
      "FY2025: consolidated revenues of $10.32B (-2% YoY) and net income of $1.34B (-20% YoY); homebuilding gross margin declined to 21.2% (from 23.7%).",
      "Backlog as of Dec 31, 2025 declined to 8,448 homes and ~$4.01B in dollar backlog (-15% units / -16% dollars).",
      "Management commentary in the FY2025 Form 10-K indicates expected pressure on gross margins during the first half of 2026 and a significant decline in revenues in Q1 2026 tied to weak orders in Q3 2025 and strong backlog turnover in Q4 2025."
    ],
    "investment_highlights": [
      "Base-case revenue profile assumes a near-term normalization after 2026 volume pressure, followed by gradual growth as the backlog digestion stabilizes.",
      "Base-case EBIT margin rebounds from the 2025 compression level toward a long-run target as pricing/mix and lot-cost dynamics normalize (with conservative terminal economics).",
      "Reinvestment intensity is modeled with low capex and D&A as a percentage of sales, consistent with NVR\u2019s capital discipline and subcontractor-based construction model.",
      "Net working capital intensity is stabilized after the 2025 contract land deposit build, reflecting NVR\u2019s lot-control and customer deposit mechanics.",
      "Terminal valuation uses a modest perpetual growth rate aligned to long-run U.S. housing demand and margin mean reversion."
    ],
    "key_risks": [
      "A prolonged housing downturn (weaker demand and/or higher cancellations) extending revenue pressure and keeping margins depressed.",
      "Unexpected increases in land/lot costs and/or additional contract land deposit impairments.",
      "Mortgage banking earnings volatility from loan production changes and secondary market pricing/gains."
